icy Confidence Score","global.order":"Order","home.ec2.cwagent.procstat.form.add.monitor":"Add EC2 CloudWatch Agent Procstat","msp.customer.downgrading":"Downgrading...","vmware.vdi.hv.max.active.conn":"Max. Acive Connections","hetznercloud_server.image_architecture":"Image Architecture","practice.oci.security.list.unrestricted.stateless.outbound":"OCI Security List - Unrestricted Stateless Outbound Traffic","azure.vnetconnection.thresholds":"VNet Connection Thresholds","oci.obc.ldap.conn.errors.avg":"Avg LDAP Connection Errors","practice.aws.neptune.managed.kms.keys.info.desc":"<p><b>Baseline : <\/b><\/p><p> Checks if your Amazon Neptune database instances are encrypted using KMS Customer Master Keys (CMKs) instead of AWS managed-keys (default encryption keys used by the service when there are no customer keys defined).<\/p><p><b>Description : <\/b><\/p><p> Upon defining and using your own KMS CMK customer-managed keys to protect the Neptune database instances, you gain full control over who can use these keys to access the data (including the system metadata). The AWS KMS service allows you to create, rotate, disable and audit CMK encryption keys for your file systems and helps you have more granular control over your data-at-rest encryption/decryption process.<\/p><p><b>Recommendation : <\/b><\/p><p> Encrypt an existing AWS Neptune database instances, you need to re-create instance with the required encryption configuration.<\/p>","memory_locked":"Locked Memory","profile.downloadtime.from.secondary":"Download time threshold for secondary locations","network.device.test.credential.passed":"Passed","alibabacloudapsaradblindorm.queryrateopsmetrics":"Query Rate & Ops Metrics","alibabacloud_apsaradb_tair.tps":"Transactions Per Second","bgp.solution.retry.collection":"Retry data collection - temporary API issue","bgp.chart.prefix.count":"Prefix Count","sdn.arubaedgeconnect.group.id":"Group","zia.guardrail.profile.form.topic.restrictions.section":"Topic Restrictions","e